      "math_academy_id": "e-11000",
      "problem": "If $\\sin x = \\dfrac{1}2,$ then what is $-2\\sin(x+6\\pi)-3?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y = \\sin x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\sin{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2\\cdot 3\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+6\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ then $\\sin{(x+6\\pi)} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+6\\pi )-3 & =-2(\\frac{1}{2})-3 \\\\  & =-1-3 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
